\title{Directed graph}
\date{2026-09-24T15:22:14Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\taxon{Definition}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\taxon{Definition} \p{A \em{directed graph} is just like an
[undirected graph](00LN), except that the edges are \em{ordered} pairs
of vertices, #{E \subseteq V \times V}.  This means that each edge has
a particular \em{direction}: an edge goes \em{from} one vertex \em{to}
another vertex instead of just connecting two vertices.}

\p{We typically draw directed graphs by putting arrowheads on the
edges to indicate which direction they go.}

